          "committer": {
            "email": "noreply@github.com",
            "name": "GitHub",
            "username": "web-flow"
          },
          "distinct": true,
          "id": "73d9c7cd5f9393bcbecf92659120cf8340fe7a52",
          "message": "fix(velvet)!: draw particle trails, tint, blend and sorting, render VFX Graph, and let a positive UseFrame priority take SceneView rendering over (#1449)\n\n## What changed\n- **Particles:** each system's renderer bakes its geometry (mesh particles, stretched billboards, texture-sheet frames, trails — including trails-only systems in render mode None) and draws it in the element. Material tint (`_BaseColor` / `_TintColor` / `_Color`), additive and multiply blending (through UI Toolkit custom-material layers) and cross-system sorting (sorting layer, order in layer, render queue, depth plus `sortingFudge`) follow the scene. External sub-emitters are cloned at their own world pose.\n- **VFX Graph:** new `V.Particles(VisualEffect, …)` overload, rendered by a private camera into a RenderTexture drawn by the element.\n- **SceneView:** the camera output is a child that stays beneath every other child (including portalled rows) instead of the element's background image; SceneViews can share a camera.\n- **UseFrame:** a positive-priority callback holds each live SceneView camera in its panel disabled, as react-three-fiber's root skips its render; held cameras are restored before an assembly reload and a scene save.\n- URP is the only tested pipeline; the docs say so.\n\nBreaking changes are recorded in `Changelog~/breaking/fix-1127-particles-useframe.md`.\n\n## Docs\n`particles.md`, `scene-view.md`, `player-builds.md`, `Documentation~/README.md`.\n\nCloses #1127\n\n---\n_Generated by [Claude Code](https://claude.ai/code/session_01FoK7YxdRjMGmfmRkEEmS88)_",
          "timestamp": "2026-10-23T11:04:30Z",
