          "distinct": true,
          "id": "c63a2ec039ee0dedfc945456717f47ebff1197e3",
          "message": "refactor(velvet): schedule the core through a host scheduler and UseFrame through a frame clock (#1714)\n\nRefs #1659. The core now schedules through a host scheduler, and `UseFrame` subscribes through a frame clock, instead of calling `VisualElement.schedule` and the panel directly. There is no public API change and no behaviour change.\n\n## What changed\n\n- **`IHostScheduler`** offers `Schedule`, `Enter`/`Exit`/`Run`, `CurrentPass` and `InPassOf`. `PanelHostScheduler` is its UI Toolkit implementation, a stateless singleton over the unchanged `PanelSchedulerCallback` statics. A neutral `HostPass` replaces `PanelSchedulerCallback.Outer`, so no `IPanel` type appears in the core.\n- **The scheduler lives on the batch scheduler**, which `ReconcilerContext.HostScheduler` exposes. A fiber with no reconciler falls back to the panel scheduler through `HostSchedulers.For(fiber)`.\n- **The core's 8 `schedule.Execute` sites** now go through the host scheduler, on the same element and in the same position. They are in `FiberBatchScheduler` (2), `FiberWorkLoop` (2), `FiberEffects` (2), `FiberRenderer` and `FiberCommitWork`. Each closure site keeps its early return for a null `MountPoint`, so no closure is allocated where none was before.\n- **`IFrameClock` and `IFrameSubscription`**, with `PanelFrameClock` as the panel implementation, exposed as `ReconcilerContext.FrameClock`. `UseFrame` now subscribes through the clock. The attach and detach callbacks and the per-panel dispatcher moved into `PanelFrameClock`, and `UseFrameDispatcher` is untouched, so the tick order, sort and dt clamp run the same code.\n- **Guard.** `HostSchedulerBoundaryTests` uses Cecil to scan the five core types and their nested types. It fails on `VisualElement.schedule`, on `VisualElement.panel`, or on any use of `IPanel` or `PanelSchedulerCallback`.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- **Guard.** Adding `fiber.MountPoint.schedule.Execute(() => { })` to `FiberCommitWork.ScheduleParkedPassResume` turns the guard red. The base cannot build the guard, so this perturbation stands in for a base-red run.\n- **Targeted EditMode runs:**\n  - EventLog, frame-scheduling, time-sliced, passive-drain, `UseFrame`, parked-transition and transition-tier fixtures, and `HostCommitRoutingTests`: 215 of 215 pass.\n  - 19 more fixtures, among them scene view, motion clock and playback, animation sequence, ring, backdrop and filter, and `RenderCommitAllocationTests`: 671 of 672 pass.\n  - The one failure is `BackdropFilterLayerTests…EdgeBlursOnlyInsideTheBox`, a screen capture that reads uniform grey. It has also failed on other branches on the same machine.\n- `compile_check.py` and the cheap guards pass. The refusal baseline is unchanged.\n\n## Left for later\n\n- **The G7 benchmark**, which would measure the production drain on a live panel, is not added.\n- **Test fakes.** `UseFrameFakeClockHost` and `HeldMotionClock` move to an `IFrameClock` fake with the TestRenderer (#1663).\n- **Other panel readers outside the five types**, such as `FiberLayoutReflow`'s anchor read, the focus code and `DndActiveDrag`, are the host interface's to route (#1660).\n- **`UseFrameDispatcher.TakesOverRendering(IPanel)`** is still read by `SceneViewDriver`.\n\nNo CHANGELOG fragment: the change is internal.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)",
