          "distinct": true,
          "id": "94b37dcb42099eb37eefd3321a79a9392ced1652",
          "message": "perf(velvet): read a keyed reorder's positions from the row model (#1716)\n\nRefs #1696. Step 6 of the row model. A keyed reorder now reads the model, instead of searching the live child list and walking physical indices. No behaviour changes.\n\n## What changed\n\n- **`HostCommitQueue.Reorder`** finds each moved child and its right neighbour through `ChildListWrites.PhysicalIndexOf`. `IndexOfNear` and the two search hints are gone. The move itself is still a remove followed by an insert.\n- **`ChildElementPlacement.ComputeLisAnchors`** reads the range's rendered children in one `ChildListWrites.Rows` call, into a list pooled in `ReconcilerBufferPool`. It used to walk physical indices, skipping `IsSpacer`.\n- **`PlacesOutOfCreationOrder`** asks the model for each kept child's index, where it used to table every child of the container. It is now static.\n- **Risk R4.** The old scan skipped `IsSpacer` while other readers skip `IsInvisible`. The new read takes rendered rows only, so it skips every invisible child the same way. The LIS compares positions only with each other, so its anchors are unchanged.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- **`KeyedReorderRowReadScalingTests`** rotates the last 8 of 1,000 and of 2,000 children, and requires the reads to grow less than 1.5×. On step 4's head they grow 1.989×, so it fails there; here it passes.\n- **`ZIndexStackingTests`** gains a characterization: keyed rows with a back-band and a front-band element among them, reversed. It holds the order `r0,r4,r3,[r2],r1,[r0],r2`.\n- **Targeted EditMode, all passing, none inconclusive:** placement, scaling, `HostCommitQueueAllocationTests`, `CommitWorkCostTests`, the keyed-list, z-index and z-layer fixtures, presence, slot and every EventLog fixture.\n- **Static checks.** `compile_check.py` passes. The refusal baseline lowers `ChildElementPlacement.cs` from 2 to 1.\n\nNo CHANGELOG fragment: the change is internal.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)",
          "timestamp": "2026-11-18T13:20:08+09:00",
