          "message": "fix(velvet): paint a clipping element's dashed border and divider on one overlay as two layers (#1777)\n\nCloses #1629.\n\n`FaceBorderOverlay` kept one overlay per element. A clipping child of a `divide-dashed` (or `divide-dotted`) parent that also carries a dashed border or a shadow had two layers syncing that one overlay:\n- `Ensure` replaced the paint, so the layer that synced last won.\n- The divider's `ClipsAndSettle` read the border's overlay as its own and never settled, so its edge was painted by nothing.\n\n## What changed\n\n- The overlay holds a `Layer` (`Face`, `Divider`) with one paint per layer and paints Face, then Divider, in one `generateVisualContent`.\n- `Has`/`Ensure`/`Sync`/`Remove`/`RemoveLater`/`ClipsAndSettle` take a layer, Face by default. The divider passes `Divider`.\n- `Remove` clears only its layer. The overlay leaves the hierarchy once no layer holds a paint.\n- `HasOverlay` answers for any layer, and `RingOverlay` uses it to place its band after the overlay.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- `FaceBorderOverlayDividerSharingTests` uses only the base's API, so CI's base-red can measure it. Its two cases are red on the base:\n  - With the border's overlay up, a divider on the clipping element queues its own settle.\n  - Detaching the divider leaves the border's overlay.\n- `FaceBorderOverlayLayerTests` covers the new API, so these cases cannot compile on the base:\n  - each layer's paint is asked, the face before the divider;\n  - removing one layer keeps the other;\n  - removing both leaves no overlay;\n  - a removed layer's paint is not asked again.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)",
          "timestamp": "2026-11-18T13:11:22Z",
