          "distinct": true,
          "id": "c0814043f9af32846982399d05618666ae1b79a2",
          "message": "fix(velvet): carry a class-timed transition taken over before its run event on the class's curve (#1778)\n\nCloses #1593.\n\n`StyleEaseCurve` replaces a class-timed transition's curve with Tailwind's only when the transition's `TransitionRunEvent` reaches it. UI Toolkit dispatches that event at the end of the next `UpdateAnimations`, after the scheduler runs. A layoutId crossfade, or a rotate, translate, scale or radius take-over (`RunningStyleTransition.TakeOver`), that ran in that window copied UI Toolkit's curve and carried the rest of the transition on it. The issue expected that telling a pending run event apart would need an engine member. State Velvet keeps itself is enough.\n\n## What changed\n\n- `StyleEaseCurve.Binding` records:\n  - `RunningAtListen`: the transitions running when its listener was registered;\n  - `Run`: the id and start of each transition `OnRun` has seen.\n  Both are cleared when the listener is unregistered, and pool reset unregisters it.\n- `RunningStyleTransition.Retime` returns the id and start of the transition it found.\n- `TakenOver` hands a transition in neither record through the same mapping `OnRun` applies, so the carry runs on the class's curve. The tween path is unchanged.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- `StyleEaseCurveTakeOverTests` (new): five cases, one each for opacity, rotate, translate, scale and radius. Each takes over a one-second `ease-in-out` transition before its run event and reads Tailwind's curve at 0.3 progress (≈ 0.367) rather than InOutQuad (0.18). Red on the base.\n- Two controls are green on the base, and each has a cut that reddens it:\n  - a transition running as the element began to be listened to keeps its curve;\n  - a transition whose run event was already seen keeps the curve it was given.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)",
          "timestamp": "2026-11-18T13:24:23Z",
