          "distinct": true,
          "id": "4702884bf705cfae4bd4070a0ade0c5563641b07",
          "message": "fix(velvet): read a ring colour's opacity modifier variable and an untyped non-colour bracket as Tailwind 4.3.3 does (#1793)\n\nCloses #1787\n\nAn untyped ring bracket holding no colour and no length (`ring-2 ring-[10%]/50`, `ring-2 ring-[foo]/50`) behind an\nopacity modifier is now read as the colour Tailwind mixes it into, which the box-shadow rejects, so the ring is\ndiscarded as a typed bracket's is. `MixesNonColour` judged only a keyword or a typed value; it now asks whether a\ncolour reader rejects the value, with a length excluded since that is the width's.\n\nThe issue's first item claimed that a modifier reading a variable is never resolved. That is not what the code does: the\nvariable is substituted through the consumer path a bracket's goes through, and a resolved percentage reaches\n`MixesNonColour` as a literal modifier. Those rows are pinned by mounted cases declared characterization, green on the\nbase. What did differ is a variable that resolves to a non-percentage (`[--a:0.5] ring-[red]/(--a)`): it was treated as\nundeclared and drew the text-colour ring, where the browser draws none, because `--tw-ring-color` carries the invalid\n`color-mix()` through and the box-shadow substituting it is invalid at computed-value time. It now discards the\nbox-shadow; an undeclared variable keeps the text-colour ring. A literal `ring-[red]/[0.5]` is a fraction Tailwind\nconverts to 50% and still draws red at 50%, so the variable and literal spellings of 0.5 differ by design.\nDocumentation: no guide describes this; CHANGELOG fragment added.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)",
          "timestamp": "2026-11-23T04:01:10Z",
          "tree_id": "fd554c5098019121ee84bc715ce8cc60e825ad9a",
          "url": "https://github.com/s4k10503/velvet/commit/4702884bf705cfae4bd4070a0ade0c5563641b07"
