          "id": "8ad2130bd5dc776b4edd912a9dcb32f2a4ecc57b",
          "message": "fix(velvet): render no root inside another root's work, through one execution context (#1739)\n\nRefs #1655. Stacked on #1738.\n\nVelvet rendered one root inside another root's render, commit or passive flush: a `V.Mount` from an\n`onCreated` callback, a layout effect, a `refCallback:` or a `UseEffect`; a `MountedTree.Dispose` from a layout\ncleanup; a discrete handler that a render's writes or a layout effect fired; a `V.VirtualList` range a\n`ScrollToItem` asked for there. A Portal's children and a flushed component's own body rendered while another root's\nwork was on the stack.\n\n## What changed\n\n`FiberExecutionContext` is React's `executionContext`: one flag for every root, set while an entry renders,\ncommits or flushes passive effects. Each entry is split into a door, which keeps today's name and is what the\npanel scheduler, a UI Toolkit callback, the main-thread pump or a public or test entry calls, and a body,\nmost of them named `...Work`, which Velvet's own render and commit code calls. A door reached while the flag is clear runs its\nbody as the outermost entry. Reached while it is set, the request is held, except a flush of a scheduler whose\nown drain is on the stack, which that drain takes. What is held runs once the outermost entry ends, in the\norder it was asked for: before each item, the pending passive effects of the root whose work ran last; once the\nqueue is empty, the sync-lane work any scheduler owes, held work or not. Each item runs in its own try, and\nwhat it throws is logged. A root whose own work threw during the entry gets no more rendering in that release.\n- Its drain stands down in `DrainImmediateWork`, and also in the `FlushState` door, which does not drain.\n- A held VirtualList range, async resolve or resume of that root is handed to a later frame of that root instead of running. The range renders from where the scroller is then.\n- A held unmount of that root still runs.\n- Its queued updates render at its next update drain, as before. Where one of them is sync-lane work, the first render, commit or `UseEffect` flush of any root to end after control has returned also renders it. A request that one root makes a 51st time in one release (the same kind and key) is skipped for the rest of that release, and \"Maximum update depth exceeded\" is logged once for it. That root's other requests still run.\n\nA VirtualList range's door is `UpdateVisibleRange`: the scroll and geometry callbacks' own bookkeeping runs at\nonce, so a `ScrollToItem` keeps the target the content's layout reaches. A failure delivered to a boundary with\nno entry on the stack, a `UseFrame` callback's, opens one for the fallback swap.\n\nThe render, reconcile, resume, layout-commit, passive-flush and range steps assert that an entry is on the\nstack. `scripts/test_quality/entry_sites_check.py` fails when a member that the guard does not exempt by its\nqualified name references a door, and it fails on an exemption that no longer matches anything. The guard and\nits suite run in `Test ▸ test-quality`.\n\nAn unmount pass of another root no longer begins inside a pass. A deletion's teardown still runs its own root's unmount pass where the pass applies its writes.\n\n## Behaviour that changes\n\nThe fragment `fix-commit-hold-nested-renders.md` states what a user sees. Outside it, the test helpers change: when `FlushStateForTest`, `FlushEffectsForTest`, `DrainForTest`, `DrainImmediateForTest`, or a bare `Reconcile` or `ContinueReconcile` is called from inside a callback, it now runs its work at the outermost exit instead of where it is called. Each step of `FlushStateForTest` is now also an entry, so a root that owes the sync lane has that work and its passive effects run there.\n\n## Not covered\n\n- A component that throws uncaught on every commit and re-queues itself can make its root throw every frame. Main has the same loop in other shapes (#1747).\n- An update that one root's commit makes on another root still takes the Normal lane, where React gives it the sync lane (#1743).\n\n## Evidence\n\n`ExecutionContextEntryTests`, `HeldWorkReleaseTests`, `VirtualListScrollPlacementTests`, `PassOwnershipTests`\nand `DeletionAtOwnerTurnTests` hold where each request renders and how the release runs;\n`test_entry_sites_check.py` holds the guard.\n\n`HeldWorkReleaseTests` cannot build on the base. Neither can the cases that run through `FiberExecutionContextTestAccess`: `MotionScheduledMechanicsTests`, `ComponentStateReparityTests`, `InlineExplicitPositionCacheTests`, `PassBufferPoolReturnTests` (three cases) and `TimeSlicedFiberTests`. The base therefore cannot show these cases red. Their failures are measured against cuts of the release by a mutation run on this branch, and its verdicts will be linked here.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)",
