          "distinct": true,
          "id": "fec6da397d35ec47c65c05d437abd741bf9b80c5",
          "message": "refactor(velvet): render a wave's components before any of their writes apply (#1754)\n\nRefs #1600. Refs #1655. Stacked on #1752.\n\nReact renders the updates a root takes in one render and commits them once. Velvet's drain gave each dirty component a pass of its own, and each pass applied its child-list writes and ran its deletions' cleanups and its hook commit before the next component rendered; a drain's insertion and layout effects already waited for its end. This change makes a drain one wave: the components it renders join one pass, the writes those renders record apply when the wave ends, and the wave then commits. A flush, a mount and a resumed time-sliced render are waves of their own. It is step B of the commit-phase design for #1600's remaining half.\n\n## What changed\n\n- **`FiberWave`** (new). `Open` marks the wave and holds the pass journal. `End` finishes the pass, closes the journal, routes the failures its writes threw, and refreshes the has- variants. `Commit` runs the drain-end work: the deferred layout effects, the drag-overlay join and the layoutId pass.\n- **Four doors open a wave; work inside one joins it.** The FlushState, RenderMounted, `V.Mount` and resume doors open a wave. A drain member or an owed render joins the open one (`BeginOrJoinPass`).\n- **A failed insert starts where React's commit starts it.** `FiberNodeFactory.CreateElement` records the component that created each element, and the Portal it sits in, while a wave records (`ReconcilePass.Owners`). A failed op's search start is fixed when it is filed (`ChildListWrites.FileFailure`, `PlaceRelocated`, the Portal drain): the component that created the element if a commit has taken it (`ComponentFiber.HasCommitted`, set where a commit's layout pass reaches a fiber that is not offscreen), otherwise its nearest ancestor that one has, stopping at the component that rendered the Portal the element sits in and at the mount root. React captures a placement's error from the return of the topmost fiber new in the commit (`commitHostPlacement`), and places a portal's children one by one. Any other failed op starts at its segment's root. `End` routes each as a commit-phase capture, and each failed z-* placement is filed; none is skipped. A failure whose element was on a panel when its op threw and is off one once the wave's writes have applied is not reported.\n- **A Portal's or WorldSpace's mount ends the pass.** `FinishTopLevelPass` applies what has been recorded when a render queued such a mount, because the mount's drain runs inside that render. A throw from a Portal's children is filed under the component that rendered the Portal, and the drain goes on to the next queued mount.\n- **Deletion marks.** A wave's deletion can mark a fiber `IsBeingDeleted` and leave its disposal to where the wave applies the teardown it recorded, so the boundary search, `ShowCaughtFallback` and `ReconcileRenderedTree` stop at that mark as they did at `IsDisposed`.\n\n## Behaviour that changes\n\nThe fragment `refactor-one-commit-per-wave.md` states two changes:\n\n- A component a transition's last work owes a render commits with that work, so the refs it replaces detach before that component's layout effects run.\n- An error a new element's panel-attach callback throws while a wave applies its writes goes to a boundary searched for from where React's commit starts that placement's search, and is not reported where the element is off the panel again once those writes have applied.\n\nThe wave also makes a component later in a batch read an earlier one's elements and its removed components' cleanup writes as they were before the batch. The fragment does not state that yet, because a VirtualList host's render (#1720), a deletion inside it (#1655) and a Portal's or WorldSpace's mount (#1761) still apply their writes during the wave; it is stated once those land. `react-migration.md`'s error-boundary row gives where an attach error goes; `styling-pointer-events.md` gives the pointer-events walk's new timing.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- **`OneCommitPerWaveTests`** and **`WaveApplyFailureTests`**. Each case is red on the base, or declares `GREEN_ON_BASE` with its reason.\n- **`RefLayoutInterleaveTests`**: one case re-expected. React detaches a replaced ref in the mutation phase, before any layout effect of the same commit.\n- **`MotionScheduledMechanicsTests`**: the pool hand-over case now hands the element over across two drains, since a removal no longer applies before the next mount of the same wave.\n\n## Not covered\n\n- **A failed removal or move** is filed under its segment's root (#1760), and so is a removal a boundary's own fallback swap makes (#1763).\n- **A settle render's layout effect.** A component a transition both updates and owes a settle render runs neither half of a layout effect whose dependency the transition changed (#1759). This change does not fix it.\n- **has- variants after a scroll** stay stale (#1758).\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)",
