          "distinct": true,
          "id": "fd63b7a219275a1957d5128a0875ea54911f65e5",
          "message": "perf(velvet): read a class array's families without copying a token (#1811)\n\nCloses #1799.\n\n`StyleClassFamilies` caches which utility families a class array holds, and on a miss `Read` runs every family predicate over every token. Two kinds of predicate copied the token to read it:\n\n- `FiberZLayerCoordinator.IsStackingToken` took the utility after the last variant separator with `Substring`, one copy per variant-prefixed token.\n- Nine predicates stripped an important bang through `StripImportant`, which builds a string: Skew, Shadow, Backdrop, Gap, Divide, PointerEvents, ZIndex, Order and Stacking.\n\nOn the benchmark #1799 tracks, a measurement on a diagnostic branch put the whole step in the stacking settle that #1722 moved to the commit. There, `FiberZLayerCoordinator.OnSettled` reads the family table for the row's arrays after every row's class passes have run, and it misses where the old in-walk read hit. Each miss copied the row class's seven `dark:` payloads: 616 blocks over 100 rows, the same count `ApplyLayoutManipulators` pays on the same misses.\n\nThe predicates now classify from spans:\n\n- `StyleArbitraryValueResolver.ImportantCore(ReadOnlySpan<char>)` returns the core `StripImportant` produces, as a slice of the token. A bang behind variants (`hover:!x`) is returned with the token unchanged, which is exact for the colon-free names and prefixes every caller compares against.\n- `StripImportant` takes its edge-bang rule from it.\n- `VariantsLength` takes a span.\n\nA miss on the family table now allocates nothing. Behaviour was compared between the base and branch assemblies over about 18 million generated tokens (variant prefixes, brackets and parentheses holding `:` and `!`, a bang at either edge or behind variants, signs, slashes, empty strings), with no difference.\n\n`ClassReadAllocationTests` counts GC.Alloc blocks over a miss on six rows: the benchmark's row class, `!bg-red-500`, `bg-red-500!`, `hover:!bg-red-500`, `!shadow-md` and `!inset-ring`. It reads a fresh copy of the array in each window, so every read misses, and asserts zero.\n\nThis PR's Benchmarks run measured the case at a median of 1105 GC.Alloc blocks (25 samples), against 2295 on main and about 1730 before #1722.\n\nThis does not change how often the table misses. On this benchmark about nine in ten per-element arrays miss each pass; #1810 tracks that.\n\nNo documentation describes this code; the changelog fragment is under `Changelog~/unreleased/`.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)",
