          "distinct": true,
          "id": "3fb2ce25a9c8047b10787c9a4b59614aea5f57c9",
          "message": "fix(velvet): cancel a switch-only transition such as justify-content's unless transition-discrete applies (#1814)\n\nRefs #1802. Closes #1808.\n\nUI Toolkit switches a property it can only switch halfway through a transition (`ValuesDiscrete<T>.Lerp` is `t < 0.5 ? a : b`). So under `transition-all`, a bare `duration-*` or any list naming such a property, a `justify-start` → `justify-end` change held the old value for half the duration. CSS does not transition a discretely animated property unless `transition-behavior: allow-discrete` applies, so it changes at once.\n\n- **Cancel at the start.** `StyleTransitionBehavior` registers one `TransitionRunEvent` callback on each root Velvet renders into:\n  - the element a tree is mounted on;\n  - a layer or world-space panel's root;\n  - each row a portal places in its target.\n\n  When UI Toolkit starts a transition of a property in `LandedAtOnce` on an element where allow-discrete does not apply, the callback cancels it through the engine's `CancelAnimation`, which writes the end value. The trigger can be a class change, an inline write or a V.Motion tween. `LandedAtOnce` is the set the engine animates only discretely, minus `visibility`, `background-position-x/y` and the font properties. `TransitionLandedAtOnceSetTests` derives that set from the engine's animation dispatch and pins `LandedAtOnce` against it.\n- **What stays as it was.** `transition-all` still names `all`, so timing lists, overrides and every other transition behave as before.\n  - CSS transitions `visibility` and `background-position` by default.\n  - A single rule cannot be right for both a font family change and a weight change; #1802 tracks the font part.\n- **`transition-discrete` / `transition-normal` are added.** They decide whether a start is cancelled, from the element's ranked classes, a play's included; the later-ranked of the two wins. A TextField places both on its input box as well as the control.\n\nThe cancel runs in the engine's next animation update. A class or tween change therefore paints its old value for the one frame that started the transition. An inline write made before that frame's animation update paints none.\n\nDocumentation: the \"Discrete properties\" section of `Documentation~/motion.md`, the `Documentation~/README.md` index and the root README.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)",
          "timestamp": "2026-11-23T23:20:01Z",
