          "distinct": true,
          "id": "4c50ee756fa206097c4b176f45a1b9e5dbf18889",
          "message": "fix(velvet): claim a leaf's key once it commits, so a boundary's fallback is not reported as a duplicate (#1816)\n\nCloses #1746.\n\nWhen an element of an error boundary's output threw while it was being created or patched, the boundary's fallback could log `Duplicate key detected among new siblings` although no two siblings shared a key. Seven cases on main log it today, all as `position 0 under slot path CBF29CE484222325`.\n\n`CommitLeaf` claimed a leaf's key in `NewKeys` at its top, before `MatchOldLeaf` and the create or patch, but recorded the leaf in `Committed` only after them. `RollbackCommitTo` takes back the keys of `Committed` entries, so a leaf whose create or patch threw kept its claim. `ExpandBoundaryInline` then expanded the fallback in the same commit, and an unkeyed fallback element at position 0 of the boundary's root has the same key as a failed row there.\n\nThe duplicate check now reads `NewKeys.Contains(key)` where it did, so the warning still comes before the leaf's subtree is built, and the key is claimed beside the `Committed` entry. Every path that puts a leaf into the committed tree reaches the claim, so a key really repeated among committed siblings still warns. `NewKeys` has no reader other than that check.\n\nThe new case in `BoundaryCatchScopeTests` puts the throwing row first in a boundary's output and gives the boundary a bare-element fallback. It covers the three `CommitLeaf` branches: a create where nothing stood, a create replacing a label, and a patch. On the base the fallback logs the warning, and on this branch it does not.\n\nNo documentation describes this warning, so no guide changes.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)",
          "timestamp": "2026-11-24T11:26:23+09:00",
          "tree_id": "c37c188d16446087aa56f561bedd5cb60f4de52a",
