          "distinct": true,
          "id": "254befc1a28ce353cf78759ad5ca2ca8909e89da",
          "message": "fix(velvet): keep a drain's queued components when an exception leaves it, and contain the StrictMode signature's ToString (#1817)\n\nCloses #1748.\n\nAn exception that left a drain of queued updates as it rendered one component took the components queued behind it off the queue without rendering them. They stayed dirty, so a later update of a lane they already held reached no tier: `ScheduleRerender` re-enrols a dirty fiber only for a lane that outranks the ones it holds. `Drain` copied the tier's order into `_drainBuffer` and cleared the order, and nothing put back what an exception left in the buffer.\n\nA component's own render error does not leave the drain: `RenderAndReconcile` hands it to an error boundary or to the root. The one route from user code found is the Editor's StrictMode diagnostic. `DoubleInvokeRenderForStrictMode` signed the first render's output with `FiberStrictMode.ComputeSignature` outside the guard around its second render, and the signature reads props, field values and memo deps through `ToString`. A value whose `ToString` throws therefore threw out of the render:\n\n- Rendered within its parent's render, the component passed the error to its parent. The nearest error boundary showed its fallback, or, with none, the tree unmounted.\n- Rendered for an update queued on it, the component threw out of the drain and stranded what was queued behind it.\n\nChanges:\n\n- `ComputeSignature` contains a throw and returns an empty signature, which no readable output signs as. An output unreadable on both renders is not reported as impure; one unreadable on one render only is reported through the existing impure-render error. The render-phase-update check is unchanged.\n- `Drain` catches, puts every still-dirty entry left in `_drainBuffer` back through `FiberWorkLoop.ScheduleFlush(fiber, fiber.LaneQueue.Min)`, and rethrows. The entry goes to the tier its lowest lane belongs to, and the tier's own intake dedupes and arms it. Both tiers drain through `Drain`, so this covers the transition tier too.\n\nTests:\n\n- `StrictModeDoubleInvokeTests` gains a case where a component's child has props whose `ToString` throws after the mount, ahead of a second component in one drain. On the base the exception leaves the drain and the second component keeps its old text.\n- `DrainFlushThrowTests` drives each tier's drain with an exception leaving it. It uses an internal signal that names no boundary, since no other exception reaches that far once the diagnostic contains its own. On the base, the component behind the thrower does not render its next update of the same priority.\n\nNo guide describes either behaviour, so no documentation changes.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)",
